@prefix pm:      <https://pajew.ski/prima-materia/ontology#> .
@prefix pmc:     <https://pajew.ski/prima-materia/concepts/> .
@prefix pmp:     <https://pajew.ski/prima-materia/practices/> .
@prefix rdfs:    <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#> .
@prefix skos:    <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#> .
@prefix xsd:     <http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#> .

#
# prima-materia — who is allowed to test a person, and on what ground the test
# is refused
#
# A protocol on pmc:TheTestOfAPersonIsRefused, which stood on the queue SPEC §16
# derives from the absence of a pm:Testing node and which this run added a fourth
# witness to. It is written because a run that takes up no examination cannot
# afterwards claim that none was pending, and because the two strands of this run
# happened to produce, from two unconnected corpora, three cases that bear on the
# statement's two qualifying clauses.
#
# The outcome is deviation in the qualifications and not in the core. The core —
# that a transmission locating a capacity in a person states a rule withdrawing
# that person from a deliberate test — held against everything read. Both
# qualifications failed, and they failed in ways that point the same direction.
#
# The counter-search ran against the holding, as SPEC §16 requires, and what it
# turned up is below rather than left out. It is not a search of modern research:
# nothing here enters through pm:evidenceFrom, because the claim is a regularity
# in what transmissions say and is examined against texts.
#

pmp:WhoMayTestAPersonExamination a pm:Testing ;
    rdfs:label "Whether the refusal of a test is grounded in the capacity and not in the asker"@en, "Ob die Verweigerung einer Prüfung im Vermögen und nicht im Fragenden begründet ist"@de ;
    skos:definition "A reading protocol on the claim that where a transmission locates a claimed capacity in a person rather than in a procedure anyone could work, it states a rule withdrawing that person from a deliberate test, grounds the rule in a property of the capacity or of the party exercised through it and not in the unworthiness of whoever proposes the test, and that where two practitioners instead measure their capacities against one another it fixes a criterion and does not forbid the trial. What is claimed is a regularity in what transmissions say, so the protocol fails or holds against texts and not against practitioners."@en ;
    pm:tests pmc:TheTestOfAPersonIsRefused ;
    pm:falsifiedBy "For the core of the statement: a transmission claiming a capacity of a person whose earliest witness invites an outsider to test it, states what a negative outcome would be, and treats such an outcome as telling against the capacity rather than against the conditions of the trial. That condition is the claim's own and has NOT been met. For the first qualification: a transmission of the same kind that grounds its refusal in the unworthiness of whoever proposes the test. That has been met. For the second: a deliberate trial of such a capacity, proposed from inside the transmission, that the transmission does not forbid and does not treat as a contest between equals. That has been met twice."@en ;
    pm:examinationState pm:casesWithDeviation ;
    pm:counterSearch pm:counterSearchFoundOpposition ;
    pm:counterSearchNote "Searched on 2026-09-29 across the two corpora read at the wording in this run and then across the holding. In the Swedenborgian corpus the question was put to eight volumes: whether the refusal of visible proof is grounded in the thing or in the asker. Both grounds are there and in two different works. Arcana 10751 grounds it in the thing — appearances are rare now lest they compel men to believe, for a compulsory faith such as enters by miracles does not cleave to the man — and that is the witness at pmc:SwedenborgSignaNonCoguntFidem. Heaven and Hell 456 grounds it in the asker — those who have confirmed themselves in falses would not believe the evidence of their senses, and such demonstrations are dangerous to them because they would first believe and afterwards deny — and that is pmc:SwedenborgProfanatioPostFidem. The two passages refuse the same demonstration and do not agree about why."@en ;
    pm:counterSearchNote "Second search, over the second corpus of this run, for the boundary clause. The Visuddhimagga narrates at XX.111 a deliberate trial of one practitioner's attainment by another: Dhammadinna puts a thousand questions and every one is answered, then has the elder Mahanaga create an elephant, has him drive it at himself, the elder flinches and runs, and the flinch establishes that an attainment of sixty years' standing was not what its holder took it for. The node is pmc:UpakkilesaHatthiParikkha. The trial is narrated approvingly and is not forbidden, and the doctrinal examination that preceded it established nothing while the involuntary reaction established everything."@en ;
    pm:counterSearchNote "Third search, over the holding rather than over a corpus, asking whether the boundary clause already had more cases than the one it names. It did not name them and it has them. Before this run the clause rested on the Sami drum contest, two practitioners painting reindeer to see whose wins. This run adds two, and neither fits the wording: the Swedenborgian test at pmc:SwedenborgProbatioSpiritus is put by a practitioner to his interlocutor and not to a second practitioner, and the Theravada test is put by one practitioner to another who is his senior and his own former teacher, with correction and not ranking as the outcome. So the clause now carries three cases of three different shapes, and what they share is not that the parties are equals but that the person proposing the test stands inside the transmission."@en ;
    pm:examinedBy "prima-materia, 2026-09-29; the protocol is a reading of the two corpora opened in this run plus a search of the holding, and the falsifiers of the two qualifications were formulated before the cases were looked for" ;
    pm:protocolUpdated "2026-09-29"^^xsd:date ;
    skos:note "What holds, and it is the part worth having. In everything read the core of the statement was confirmed: no transmission in this holding that locates a capacity in a person invites an outsider to test it and states what a negative outcome would be. The Swedenborgian corpus is the strongest confirmation yet, because it is the one corpus that had the least need of such a rule — its author was believed by nobody in particular and had no lineage to protect — and it refuses the demonstration twice over anyway, at some length, and adds that a demonstration which succeeded and was afterwards denied carries the lowest hell."@en ;
    skos:note "What fails, first qualification. The statement says the rule is grounded in a property of the capacity or of the party exercised through it AND NOT in the unworthiness of whoever proposes the test. One corpus does both, in two works, and neither passage marks the difference. The word that has to change is the negation: not, becomes not only. That is a real loss, because the excluded ground was doing work — a transmission that blames the asker has said something about persons rather than about the capacity, and the statement's interest was that these transmissions do not take that easy road. The finding is that one of them takes both roads."@en ;
    skos:note "What fails, second qualification, and the failure is more interesting than the first. The last sentence of the statement exempts the case of two practitioners measuring their capacities against one another. With three cases that exemption is no longer a boundary but a second class, and the three do not share the property the wording names. In the Sami case the parties are equals and the outcome is a rank. In the Theravada case they are not equals and the outcome is a correction that the loser accepts and acts on within the hour. In the Swedenborgian case the party tested is not a practitioner at all but the interlocutor whose identity is at issue. What all three share is that the test is proposed from inside the transmission by somebody who wants to know, and what the three original witnesses share is that the test is proposed from outside by somebody who wants to be convinced."@en ;
    skos:note "The successor statement this protocol recommends, written here and deliberately not written into the graph, because a claim is corrected where it is examined and a tidied sentence would hide the cases. It should be about WHO may test rather than about what the refusal is grounded in: where a transmission locates a capacity in a person, a test proposed by an enquirer or an onlooker is refused, while a test proposed from inside the transmission is admitted, and it is admitted whether the parties are equals or not. If that holds, what these rules protect is not the capacity from examination but the boundary of the transmission, and the ground given for the refusal — the thing or the asker — is then a rhetorical choice and not the structure. That is a sharper claim than the present one, it is easier to break, and it can be broken by a single transmission that refuses a test proposed by one of its own."@en ;
    skos:note "Why the outcome is cases with deviation and not a refutation. The claim's own falsifier is the core and it was not met; what was met are the two qualifications, which the claim had added to save itself from cases it already knew about. A statement whose qualifications fail while its core holds is not broken, it is under-described, and the right response is a narrower successor and not a deletion. The precedent this holding has for the other outcome is examinations/the-sign-and-the-counterfeit.ttl, where the falsifier itself was met and the claim fell."@en ;
    skos:note "A limit of this protocol, stated because it decides what the next one should do. Three of the four witnesses of the claim now have a documented route between them — the Swedenborgian corpus is upstream of both the magnetic and the spiritist literature — so the confirmation of the core rests on two independent lines and not four: that block, and the Pali discourse. The Theravada case that breaks the second qualification comes from the same transmission as one of those two lines, which makes it a cheap find and a strong one at once: the transmission that refuses the outsider's test narrates an insider's test approvingly, in the same corpus, four chapters apart."@en .
